{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~","id":1369156,"title":"Scary scary, gender roles are scary ~","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-20T10:34:15-08:00","id":1369156,"title":"Scary scary, gender roles are scary ~","content":"<div align=\"center\"><img src=\"https://cdn.discordapp.com/attachments/498704231407288320/680102390489546813/image0.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div> <br /> So a discussion about Ex2 Ligier being embarrassed about Malfeas dancing since it's a feminine activity makes me wonder about gender roles in Creation. So the Bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara is originally a male figure, however, as Buddhism travels East, they become more androgynous and ends up as the female deity Guanyin due to their portfolio including compassion and kindness being the main traits, and thus more feminine.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://cdn.discordapp.com/attachments/488381373988732939/680108018951520279/833px-Nyoirin_Kannon_Nara_National_Museum_croppedf.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> For a more modern example of how something can go from masculine for feminine by a culture, I just recently read an article about how USA men used to be much more <a href=\"https://content.artofmanliness.com/uploads/2012/07/male2-400x637.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">intimate</a> between each other and it was seen as a normal thing by their contemporaries.<br /> <br /> And since most PCs come from a culture, what traits are considered to be masculine or feminine in their cultures? And do the PCs fit within those roles or do they go against it, or even want to change it? And because different cultures have different ideas about gender roles and traits, does this cause any drama in your Circle?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The no.1 fan of Demetheus. I also draw <a href=\"https://probably-unreliable.tumblr.com/tagged/Jenart\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted</a> things and is looking for commission works ~","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/298-jen","name":"Jen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar298_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T11:11:50-08:00","id":1369159,"title":"","content":"I mean different things happen different, although as far as the 2e Ligier being embarrassed because dancing is feminine goes the general consensus I've seen views it as being embarrassed because Malfeas has gone full on revelry. IE The embarrassment isn't that dancing has any attachments to women so much as its the equivalent of Malfeas choosing to get blackout drunk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T11:50:50-08:00","id":1369165,"title":"","content":"I once had an idea of a society where, for the military caste, gender roles differed depending on your age. Older men and younger women were the home guard and trained the soldiers for the campaigning forces, which would thus consist of younger men and older women.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T11:52:17-08:00","id":1369166,"title":"","content":"Ligier is embarrassed because Ruvelia was Theion's dance partner and for Malfeas to dance <i>as if he wasn't imprisoned and as if Ruvelia wasn't horrifically murdered in front of them</i> is an affront to their dignity.<br /> <br /> Not because &quot;dancing is feminine&quot;. Your interpretation is completely unsupported by any 2E text.<br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> I like to play Dynasts and lean <i>moderately</i> into the gender roles for that society. Cultural norms establish <i>norms</i> and whilst great heroes are rarely &quot;typical&quot; (and usually break stereotypes as part and parcel of being great heroes), I don't see value in going &quot;I'm going to play a character from fictional society X, but who doesn't share any of the cultural values of that society and acts more like real society Y.&quot; <br /> <br /> You can do that. If you like challenging gender roles, then a social game with friends can be a safe and rewarding environment for doing so. It's a valid form of entertainment (and a vehicle for education and validation). But it's not why my group plays RPGs -- we use them to socialise and distract ourselves from real life struggles. Anything too &quot;close to home&quot; makes people uncomfortable.<br /> <br /> For me, RPGs are about acting, fantasy and the thrill of puzzle solving. It's a chance to take on a new role and immerse myself in a different culture and see the world a different way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 02-20-2020, 03:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T12:01:48-08:00","id":1369168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1369165#post1369165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I once had an idea of a society where, for the military caste, gender roles differed depending on your age. Older men and younger women were the home guard and trained the soldiers for the campaigning forces, which would thus consist of younger men and older women.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oooh, still keeping gender roles but making the divider something else instead of sexes is something I gotta use.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The no.1 fan of Demetheus. I also draw <a href=\"https://probably-unreliable.tumblr.com/tagged/Jenart\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted</a> things and is looking for commission works ~","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/298-jen","name":"Jen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar298_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T14:06:50-08:00","id":1369198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~#post1369156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So a discussion about Ex2 Ligier being embarrassed about Malfeas dancing since it's a feminine activity makes me wonder about gender roles in Creation.</div> </div> </div> </div>To my understanding it wasn't a matter of Gender roles bur rather an issue with Class roles. When Ligier was embarrassed about Malfeas dancing, it had nothing to do with masculinity or femininity (in fact I don't think dancing in general is particular masculine or feminine, though certain types of dances might swing more towards one gender than other) and it wasn't with Malfeas dancing in general, but rather when Malfeas would dance with unrestrained passion. Basically when Malfeas engaged in formal dances befitting the Emperor of the Universe, Ligier was cool with that. When Malfeas would start break dancing or moshing, Ligier would get kind of embarrassed.<br /> <br /> To make a comparison, it would be like if there was a formal dance party at Buckingham Palace and Queen Elizabeth had a sort of mental breakdown, stripped off half her clothes and started moshing out and swinging her hair around and slam-dancing with people. I mean sure, there would probably be some people who thought that was funny, but I imagine a lot of her family members would probably be pretty embarrassed because that type of behavior and that style of dancing isn't the type of dancing or behavior that someone in her position as a queen should be engaging in, at least not publicly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 02-20-2020, 06:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T17:35:37-08:00","id":1369238,"title":"","content":"In my Creation, I have the areas influenced by the Immaculate Faith be gender-neutral: so long as you can satisfy the role of the position, gender doesn't really matter. Someone in the Hundred Kingdoms wouldn't bat an eye at a woman being the ruling Lord of a realm, so long as she could lead her armies and fight satisfactorily. If she couldn't, then she would be viewed as unfit for rulership, but the same would go for a man in the same position.<br /> <br /> It was the cultures outside of the Immaculate Faiths influence that had gender-defined roles in society, or were variously misogynistic/misandristic. Being the latter was one of the clear definintions of &quot;barbarian&quot; in the eyes of Immaculate faithful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T17:58:36-08:00","id":1369251,"title":"","content":"I'm suddenly thinking of that scene that feels kind of stock even if I can't think of a specific example of it where the protagonist travels to the home of a rowdy masculine supporting character, and they're greeted at the door by his brassy, boisterous, domineering wife...<br /> <br /> And it makes me wonder if anybody has ever played a scene with that dynamic in regards to the Lintha. The whole thing of how the Fathers manage the international side of the Family operations and are going to be the face of the organisation to most outsiders (who will often regard them as formidable), but maybe somebody might travel back to Bluehaven along with a Lintha ally (or captive) and see the significantly different dynamic in their home life (or needing to report to the home office, as the case may be).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-21T03:35:17-08:00","id":1369319,"title":"","content":"Most of my characters are very feminine but it\u2019s difficult to say if that\u2019s a reflection of what their home culture expects of their gender, or if that's more them being a stranger in a strange land.<br /> <br /> The only major exception to this is Poena, which is largely because she\u2019s a Delzhan woman.<br /> <br /> Essentially in her backstory she\u2019s a Bey\u2019s granddaughter which affords her status as minor nobility that doesn\u2019t really amount to much in the real world, was married off fairly young to an older man who was independently wealthy and rather controlling. He tried to make her more affectionate towards him by giving her an allowance to buy all the pretty clothes and domestic goods she wanted but she angered him when she started to invest that money in the smaller businesses she was dealing with. They argued and he struck her, something she\u2019s never been able to quite let go of even years after divorcing him mostly because it was a very formative experience of the use of physical force as a trump card. There are still not very many men she trusts to be in her life but luckily for her none of the women have exploited her damage yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-21T04:24:56-08:00","id":1369329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1369165#post1369165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I once had an idea of a society where, for the military caste, gender roles differed depending on your age. Older men and younger women were the home guard and trained the soldiers for the campaigning forces, which would thus consist of younger men and older women.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just my take: You could probably do that if the incentives are aligned right, such that defense is more prestigious for older men who have spent their time on the front line as young men, and has more opportunity to do Praetorian Guard / Attack on Titan Military Police type things. (Because the alternative is &quot;I can die on the field, but not gain the glory of command?&quot;).<br /> .<br /> Ultimately seems hard to do in the sense that having that kind of strict separation of skills and line of command seems like it would do bad things strategically. If Napoleon's a great field soldier then prohibiting him to only have authority at home, and no clear way to have recourse to a shared authority if he conflicts with field command, seems unstable, or vice versa for the female equivalent in the reverse situation. There's no way to use accumulated early life experience in the field / at home in later life.<br /> <br /> However, does seem like it would fit in with &quot;Exalted style cultural diversity&quot; where we'll nod it through with a smile and wink because we're interested enough in the social and story dynamics it can give rise to (in the best tradition of anthropologically exploratory speculative fiction) even if we're (though I'm not presuming to speak for everyone) not literally sure about how much institutions like this really could or couldn't persist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-21T06:10:33-08:00","id":1369349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1369329#post1369329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ultimately seems hard to do in the sense that having that kind of strict separation of skills and line of command seems like it would do bad things strategically.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe one of those societies whose warfare is not particularly strategic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-21T08:41:16-08:00","id":1369382,"title":"","content":"Thought I'd also share one of the more boarderline examples.<br /> <br /> Ah Lam is a princess of Y\u2019danna by marriage and the wife of Prince Hayri an arrangement she herself is perfectly content with but she chafes a little under how the Y\u2019dannans expect her to behave. Her homeland of Carathis was a place where a young noblewoman was expected to not only make tea but to cultivate the necessary plants herself to the point that \u201csample her tea\u201d is a term used for when suitors visit her.<br /> <br /> She is particularly attached to this practice and insists on growing all manner of mdicinal plants on the palace grounds while the royal family of Y\u2019danna are often seen to do little in their day to day activities due to a complex religious system in which a dual pantheon of Harmony and Discord is believed to ebb and wane from one extreme to the other. Her foreign influence upon her daughter has the powers that be expecting a reign heavy in discord.<br /> <br /> So, her culture\u2019s gender expression is a factor in the strife she\u2019s facing but obviously there\u2019s deeper problems than the royal family being pissy at her for gardening. It's a deliberate feature of her backstory that she's the kind of woman who once faced accusations of witchcraft.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T08:11:40-08:00","id":1369582,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1369251#post1369251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm suddenly thinking of that scene that feels kind of stock even if I can't think of a specific example of it where the protagonist travels to the home of a rowdy masculine supporting character, and they're greeted at the door by his brassy, boisterous, domineering wife...</div> </div> </div> </div>This feels so stock that it hurts. But I can't think of any examples. In print... maybe the Warriors Three meeting Volstag's wife. In film... I feel like every cop movie I've ever seen has used this but I can't think of a single example.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=ZUl4amon00E\" target=\"_blank\">I mean... there's this, kinda</a>.<br /> <br /> But I'm totally using this. Maybe not with Lintha.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T08:54:58-08:00","id":1369589,"title":"","content":"I was thinking of the Scotsman and his wife from <i>Samurai Jack</i>, but even they don't play it <i>quite</i>\u200b like that..","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T09:13:01-08:00","id":1369591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1369251#post1369251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm suddenly thinking of that scene that feels kind of stock even if I can't think of a specific example of it where the protagonist travels to the home of a rowdy masculine supporting character, and they're greeted at the door by his brassy, boisterous, domineering wife...</div> </div> </div> </div>What about the Lion Queen from the Witcher?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T09:17:35-08:00","id":1369593,"title":"","content":"Izumi and Sig from Fullmetal Alchemist is something like that.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://i.pinimg.com/originals/60/9f/5f/609f5f741403f65e454df61417c17a76.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The no.1 fan of Demetheus. I also draw <a href=\"https://probably-unreliable.tumblr.com/tagged/Jenart\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted</a> things and is looking for commission works ~","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/298-jen","name":"Jen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar298_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T09:27:43-08:00","id":1369594,"title":"","content":"NSFW: <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=i6prKZkSQUc\" target=\"_blank\">Subverted?</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T17:44:27-08:00","id":1369710,"title":"","content":"Gender doesn\u2019t seem to have much of an impact on my Water Aspect\u2019s upbringing, Kalire is something of a renaissance woman, cultured, highly educated and proficient in several languages but she\u2019s also a lot like her older brother.<br /> <br /> What is different is that in the south in particular she\u2019s able to attend soirees with some very powerful Dragon-Blooded noblewomen and that\u2019s the kind of space she wouldn\u2019t be invited into if she was male. Presumably the men have their own kind of informal gatherings to foster soft power, but we wouldn\u2019t really know because none of the PCs are male. She doesn\u2019t fully appreciate this because she\u2019s a skirt chasing teenager who gets to be a darling at parties with older and more mature women.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-24T10:10:05-08:00","id":1370082,"title":"","content":"In my chronicles, I use the soft bigotry of low expectations for men, and the perception that a woman can (and should) do it all, to add gender drama when needed.<br /> <br /> I let the NPCs opine on the high risk tolerance men engage in, and how that behavior is suitable, even admirable, in warriors but not in leaders who would bet the farm and come back with empty pockets. I often let NPCs, especially elders, needle and undermine women PCs about their lack of combat ability, or singing voice, or lack of legacy (read: babies) even though it is unfair to ask of a young Exalt to do it all. <br /> <br /> It is uncomfortable, and perhaps too close to home, but sometimes you just need villain NPCs to be hated on a personal level to drive the story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/10wrFXvyXnNjaFJ0XrHdIwce6rnG__0k9/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Sidereal Jumpstart</a> (3e fanwork Charmset)<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DQXwMXm8G1IEYLl-SmuFgKGugeZxQL63/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Cascades</a> (the above in flowchart form)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1103-tikor","name":"Tikor","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"826"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T00:07:34-08:00","id":1370261,"title":"","content":"My Zenith acts quite feminine, since there is this idea in his region that women are closer to the spirit world, and he was raised as a priest so he adopted quite some feminine mannerism.<br /> <br /> And I'm wondering where in Creation would such thing makes people go &quot;What a weirdo&quot;, Delzahn has quite strict gender-role so I guess Chiaroscuro would be one, and maybe many places in the West too if the whole thing about Storm Mother not tolerating beautiful women on ship is still around.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1369710#post1369710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Gender doesn\u2019t seem to have much of an impact on my Water Aspect\u2019s upbringing, Kalire is something of a renaissance woman, cultured, highly educated and proficient in several languages but she\u2019s also a lot like her older brother.</div> </div> </div> </div>This makes me think whether being on a noble let you get away more, or less with not acting like what society expects of you.<br /> <br /> Since many Confucius social rules don't pop up much in the rural villages here unlike what the upper class have to do (not <a href=\"https://canhdongtruyengiao.net/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/image003-1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">cutting your nails</a> and hair since your body belong to your parent for example), but a tight-knit community like village doesn't seem like a place where you can freely express yourself either...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The no.1 fan of Demetheus. I also draw <a href=\"https://probably-unreliable.tumblr.com/tagged/Jenart\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted</a> things and is looking for commission works ~","edited":{"userid":298,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/298-jen","user":"Jen","date":". 02-25-2020, 03:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/298-jen","name":"Jen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar298_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T04:08:19-08:00","id":1370297,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1370261#post1370261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My Zenith acts quite feminine</div> </div> </div> </div>What does that even mean?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T07:50:28-08:00","id":1370356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1370261#post1370261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This makes me think whether being on a noble let you get away more, or less with not acting like what society expects of you.</div> </div> </div> </div>It really depends on what social conventions you're trying to flaunt and what tolerance looks like to you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T08:29:31-08:00","id":1370362,"title":"","content":"Yeah, probably it's more that nobles and commoners have different cultures with different social mores.<br /> <br /> In Britain, certainly there's been periods where nobles could get away with stuff the plebs couldn't, but there's also been periods where the opposite is true. Nobles generally had much less control over who to marry (which lasted until our current Prince Charles, tbh), and had to marry much younger than commoners.<br /> <br /> There's also a matter of what level of noble you are; the richest, most powerful nobles can probably define culture to whatever they like in a way that lower-ranking nobles can't. For example, defining what's &quot;male&quot; and &quot;female&quot; fashion. Some of our kings had long hair, make-up, high heels, because they made it the fashion for men.<br /> Compare, for example, 3 generations of Kings (well, King George, his successor King William, his successor's husband Prince Albert).<br /> <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/George_IV%2C_when_Prince_of_Wales_-_Hoppner_1790-96.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikiped...er_1790-96.jpg</a><br /> <a href=\"http://3.bp.blogspot.com/-ymSbxBw2RUI/UAHdVkTqQAI/AAAAAAAAKfc/IBupNBsrQ0c/s1600/William+IV+also+Duke+of+Clarence.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://3.bp.blogspot.com/-ymSbxBw2RU...f+Clarence.jpg</a><br /> <a href=\"http://2.bp.blogspot.com/_-HE1lR1mlb8/TOyu00PoxDI/AAAAAAAADj0/qGXHDNMNW84/s1600/427px-Prince_Albert-1842.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://2.bp.blogspot.com/_-HE1lR1mlb...lbert-1842.jpg</a><br /> To me, the last of the three looks much more &quot;masculine&quot;, but that's only because each one defined what &quot;masculine&quot; looked like. A man in 1800 would have thought the first much more fitting for a King.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, their power may depend on cultural ideas.<br /> <i>Some</i> English monarchs were able to define culture around what they liked; tons had mistresses and the Bishops just kind of... didn't talk about it. Some English kings had male lovers (in a homophobic society), and mostly got away with it, but not always. If the King was unpopular in other ways, it suddenly became a big problem (King Edward's boyfriend was flat-out murdered by his nobles, and Edward ended up being killed by his wife, and their homosexuality was used as a justification, but he probably wouldn't have had a problem if he was a more popular King. His boyfriend was extremely unpopular because the King was so biased towards him, and he took advantage of that. Whereas Edward's wife got away with adultery and murder because she was a much stronger ruler. And James I is suspected by many historians of being bisexual, but he was a very powerful and popular King, so no-one attacked him over it. But he didn't try to make society generally less homophobic, actually he made it worse, so that's just escaping cultural bonds and not shaping culture generally).<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Elizabeth II managed to be a very successful female monarch (I'd argue the first successful female monarch of England, Matilda had to fight constant wars against her cousin, Jane Grey was a puppet who lasted 9 days, and Mary was dominated by her husband and father-in-law) by taking gender stereotypes and manipulating them to strengthen her, while avoiding the ones she didn't like. Turning herself into the virgin queen/wife of England itself. Presenting herself as a pure virgin girl that honourable Christian knights should defend, but then showing herself as having a &quot;masculine&quot; heart for war, like her father.<br /> <br /> So nobles always have a balance between relying on and controlling the cultural mores of their society.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-27T11:42:44-08:00","id":1370804,"title":"","content":"Gender plays a minor aspect of my game, as life in Azure impacts the lifestyle of our Eclipse (he runs a super fancy brothel), our Night (he runs a Militarized Mercantile Fleet) and three of the npcs more important to them, the Eclipse's wife (a widow of a rich landowner that she somehow kept full run of the businesses) and two Admirals: Eclipse's DB mother and the Blood-Soaked Diplomat.<br /> <br /> <br /> It's mostly in the form of technicalities in how the two pcs run their business and the occasional voice of dissent against the admirals. Minor, but notable.<br /> It's also little things like I never describing any woman without male company on the streets, but this one probably flew past their radar.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-06T11:30:56-08:00","id":1373232,"title":"","content":"It's weird for gender not to have come up when one of the PCs literally owns a brothel. Is the whole thing off-screen as a source of his Resource dots?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-06T15:38:43-08:00","id":1373274,"title":"","content":"My Eclipse is from Wu-Jian where I imagine sex is much less important than how good your kung fu is, but he has on a few occasions expressed concern over the hair colors of the women in the circle if that counts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-07T03:13:21-08:00","id":1373348,"title":"","content":"It didn't come up much in our game until the group accidentally appointed a misogynistic Chiaroscuran as leader of the Cult of the Illuminated. One of the social characters (the anarcho-feminist Eclipse or kindly priest Zenith) really needs to start degrading that intimacy already.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-07T05:11:33-08:00","id":1373359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1373232#post1373232\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's weird for gender not to have come up when one of the PCs literally owns a brothel. Is the whole thing off-screen as a source of his Resource dots?</div> </div> </div> </div>More or less. The story is not about the brothel, even if it is a major point of pride of his. It's home base and a mirror of its owner's perception of beauty, but the story focuses on other things for now.<br /> The Coral disparities are there, but there's little point in redescribing minutia on the same thing.<br /> <br /> Parallel to that, if the norm was different it'd change how his business runs, but wouldn't change that running a super fancy brothel mirrors his particular idiosyncrasies.<br /> <br /> It was a major factor when the story focused on it. It was major when detractors tried to undermine the operation. It will be major again if the story veers there.<br /> <br /> Thus it's minor. It doesn't shape that prop of the story much more than, say, which is the currency being forced by the Admiralty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-07T06:45:46-08:00","id":1373371,"title":"","content":"Just saying, sex workers being on-screen in an environment they're comforable in is an enormous opertunity to provide insight into the society's values and gender politics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-07T08:59:26-08:00","id":1373383,"title":"","content":"Couple of other games; I did a bit in my old game with gender roles vis-a-vis inheritance, where the monarchies in the area were matriarchal and women took a religious queenship role, while their husbands were warleaders and administrators. When a queen died, her husband lost power, because he lacked legitimacy. As a further complicating factor, the idea was that all the rulers were Dragonblood; yet because their bloodlines had declined, in reality they weren't. In a way, the system had developed to attract foreign (male) Dragonblood to marry in to the local power structures, or just to attract male Dragonblood from neighbouring kingdoms. This meant you had a powerful Dragonblood warrior king... but he couldn't rule without the support of his wife, because she actually knew the local power structures, and was considered a religious figure.<br /> <br /> But then in each state, the way this worked practically was different, as people twisted the culture to fit their own political aims, and the lack of actual Dragonblood. Ashen Witch, a powerful Fire Aspect sorceress, had a stupid-but-handsome puppet husband, so paying lip service to cultural mores while she wielded both religious and secular power. The eastern of these four monarchies had a mortal king and queen, but the queen's father (Ashen Witch's cousin), an Earth Aspect, ran everything day-to-day due to experience (until PCs killed him). Another one had a charismatic fake water aspect (he was a pelagathrope) king, who was eager to work with the PCs, but his wife who was more connected to local power structures was not (she had a terrible obsidian butterfly-related accident after calling the Wyld Hunt on the PCs). The final one was ruled by Stormhand, a fake Air aspect (he was an elemental-blood), but as soon as he screwed up by trying to fight the PCs, his wife divorced him (as she held the religious legitimacy, she had control of divorce) and replaced him with someone less likely to start a war with Solars.<br /> <br /> One confusing thing is that one of the PCs decided to create her own queendom and took some of the other Queen's tribes to do so. As a face-saving measure, Ashen Witch married her daughter to the PC. So the PC called herself Queen Enhenduanna. But this really irritated the local rulers, who were insistent that she was <i>King</i> Enhenduanna; yes, she was female, but her wife was of the true royal line, she had religious legitimacy, she was Queen, while Enhenduanna was a foreign sorcerer adventurer, so she was King. If Enhenduanna is King, married to the Queen, then she's accepted the local power structure and no one feels they have to declare war on a powerful sorceress with a ton of Dragons. But if she's Queen, then she's usurping the rights of the royal bloodlines to own the local lands, and that's a problem.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-07T09:08:36-08:00","id":1373386,"title":"","content":"With the game I do now, I wouldn't say gender roles are particularly important; because, tbh, I've somewhat played them down deliberately compared to the historical influences. I don't really want Kings to refuse to let the Changing Moon fight because she's a girl, or everyone to laugh at the No Moon because he does magic (and everyone knows magic is unmanly).<br /> <br /> But as my first story arc was based on L'Morte D'Arthur, it came up a bit, from the fact that everyone's furious with the Queen for cheating on the King, but most people seemed less bothered by the fact that the King had a bastard (possibly by his half-sister). Except the Queen who pointed this out when the PCs confronted her, of course.<br /> However, the No Moon did call the King on this, saying that he's an oath-breaker too, so just as bad as his wife.<br /> I felt this was ultimately fitting, as one of the main themes that Mallory is trying to get across in L'Morte D'Arthur is that actually Arthur and his male friends are corrupt, impious, adulterous, murderous, sometimes incestuous, and occasionally rapists, and thus the fall of Camelot is the result of their own sins, it's not just because of Guinevere (who comes out a bit better than previous portrayals with her adultery in). A deconstruction of the previous century's mores and values, to some extent. So I was quite happy for the PCs (mainly the No Moon) to point out that the Arthur-expy was just as bad, if not worse, as his wife.<br /> <br /> Of course, you could argue this is less about gender values and more about people letting off the regnant more than the consort, but I'm pretty sure it was originally about gender values (where senior male aristocrats can much more easily get away with lovers than female aristocrats can).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T03:41:26-07:00","id":1375246,"title":"","content":"Something else to mention regarding gender roles and how I sometimes benefit from them as a Storyteller/GM.<br /> <br /> In my experience when I see a PCs backstory they might have a strong sense of who their father is/was as an aspirational figure or cautionary tale but their mother is frequently a non-entity either absent from the text or briefly presented in a way that suggests the player considers them boring.<br /> <br /> I don't directly challenge this but it can be fun to approach the backstory from a different angle to what the player anticipated and do something with the mother that fleshes out their role or challenges perceptions of them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T03:55:48-07:00","id":1375248,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1375246#post1375246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something else to mention regarding gender roles and how I sometimes benefit from them as a Storyteller/GM.<br /> <br /> In my experience when I see a PCs backstory they might have a strong sense of who their father is/was as an aspirational figure or cautionary tale but their mother is frequently a non-entity either absent from the text or briefly presented in a way that suggests the player considers them boring.<br /> <br /> I don't directly challenge this but it can be fun to approach the backstory from a different angle to what the player anticipated and do something with the mother that fleshes out their role or challenges perceptions of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ooh. I'll have to remember this trick for the next game I run.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T04:16:42-07:00","id":1375252,"title":"","content":"It wouldn't work on my games much, my players don't really do that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T05:16:53-07:00","id":1375263,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1375248#post1375248\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ooh. I'll have to remember this trick for the next game I run.</div> </div> </div> </div>What's particularly useful is that it still works even if the mother in question is long dead, since you're not retconning their backstory so much as asking &quot;who was your mother before she became your mother?&quot; it just takes on a different tone if it's no longer possible to ask her.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T05:21:23-07:00","id":1375265,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1375263#post1375263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's particularly useful is that it still works even if the mother in question is long dead, since you're not retconning their backstory so much as asking &quot;who was your mother before she became your mother?&quot; it just takes on a different tone if it's no longer possible to ask her.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right, right.<br /> <br /> \u200b(Provided, of course, the character has a mother. Knight Raiton had two dads.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T12:56:23-07:00","id":1377558,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1369165#post1369165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I once had an idea of a society where, for the military caste, gender roles differed depending on your age. Older men and younger women were the home guard and trained the soldiers for the campaigning forces, which would thus consist of younger men and older women.</div> </div> </div> </div>How often did that arrangement result in dalliances among the homeguard, between the older men and younger women?<br /> <br /> Or is the assumption <i>already</i> that the old men are married to the young women, and the old women who enter the campaigning forces are widows of the previous generation of old men? And that the young men are bachelors trying to become distinguished old men whom future young women will want to marry?<br /> <br /> <br /> Biologically, the risk of babies being stillborn or born with defects like Down Syndrome increase after the mother reaches her 30s, and risks are pronounced at 40. Nevermind that Creation doesn't have modern health standards for most people to REACH the age of 40, let alone prenatal health care for dealing with birth complications that become more common after the mother is 25.<br /> <br /> So it's no wonder that historically, men tried to marry younger women, and young women consented to marry young, because everyone wanted pregnancies to succeed and babies to be born healthy. Indeed, this might support the idea that men are attracted to younger women as a matter of reproductive instinct.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, male fertility lasts practically forever, especially in a world where few men reach 50. A woman's ovum are all made when she's born, so even though they hibernate they all grow equally old together, but men are constantly producing new, fresh sperm.<br /> <br /> An argument for the existence of a hypergamy reproductive instinct is that prehistoric women wanted to have babies as soon as possible, before they reached the peak of their physical strength and athleticism. So these women wanted a mate that could immediately provide those things in abundance, for the women themselves and especially for a whole family.<br /> <br /> Rather than settle for young men their own age, young women would desire those few men who had proven they could survive the harsh world by growing into their prime. Men in their prime also tended to have the most resources, gathered by virtue of their experience and peak strength. Men would thus compete for women by proving themselves the strongest, the most cunning, the most capable of accumulating the resources necessary to support a family and of protecting both resources and family.<br /> <br /> Younger men could thus rarely compete with older men. This, and the fact that a society could lose a majority of its male population and still survive as long as the majority of women survived, would have meant that young men had less societal value than young women. Which of course made it easier to allow (or send) the young men to go off and die in hunting and warfare as the young men struggled to grow stronger and richer to build their value to future wives.<br /> <br /> Combine this with testosterone, which supports the growth of muscle and bone mass, induces higher metabolism for more energy production, and encourages aggression, competition, and risk-taking.<br /> <br /> Suddenly, the typical shounen-genre manga story makes a lot of biological sense. The stories usually feature a young man with virtually no societal value, struggling and struggling and struggling to become stronger, smarter, more skilled, with more resources, and with other male allies who have also proven their strength, all of them striving to reach adulthood as the most valuable male members of society. The most eligible bachelors.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, many times, these stories feature a love interest of a similar age to the male protagonist, and this young women already has much more societal value than the young man. Kurosaki Ichigo and Kuichi Rukia from <i>Bleach</i>, as an example, though there are plenty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":636,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","user":"Sunder the Gold","date":". 03-31-2020, 04:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T13:10:39-07:00","id":1377561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377558#post1377558\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nevermind that Creation doesn't have modern health standards for most people to REACH the age of 40, let alone prenatal health care for dealing with birth complications that become more common after the mother is 25.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, <i>The Realm</i> is pretty explicit that most peasants on the Blessed Isle make it to 70, and mentions more than a few times entities <i>dedicated</i> to prenatal care. Heck, mortal Dynasts often survive to age 100, thanks to their ability to prolong their lifespans with anagathic drugs.<br /> <br /> \u200bEven the rest of Creation is only worse off by dint of having their resources extracted by a big, huge empire.<br /> <br /> \u200bAs for the biological essentialism stuff, I really don't have the willpower to <i>properly </i>get into it right now, and there's better people than me to make the arguments anyway, so I'll leave it to them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 03-31-2020, 05:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T14:39:01-07:00","id":1377581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~#post1369156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And since most PCs come from a culture, what traits are considered to be masculine or feminine in their cultures?</div> </div> </div> </div>Rereading this post, it occurs to me that there's not much said on Nexus in regard to gender roles. <i>In terms of ethnicity and gender identity</i>, Nexus is pretty egalitarian. (How they handle poor people, on the other hand...)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~#post1369156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And because different cultures have different ideas about gender roles and traits, does this cause any drama in your Circle?</div> </div> </div> </div>Most of the drama between members of our Circle have been disputes over at what point killing is justified, or whether or not an Abyssal can be trusted, or the degree to which we should work with the Council of Entities.<br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T16:56:31-07:00","id":1377602,"title":"","content":"The hypothetical custom Sunder was commenting on is plainly a twist on the \u201cExpendable sex\u201d concept so it\u2019s perfectly reasonable to speculate on how the same attitude might affect marriage trends. Didn\u2019t need to go on at nearly such length about it though, especially when just as important is the part where there\u2019ll be more older men and younger women to go around in the first place thanks to their not being the ones dying on the front lines (a literal process of elimination, one might call it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","user":"Sith_Happens","date":". 03-31-2020, 09:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T17:47:39-07:00","id":1377612,"title":"","content":"Hmm, do I want to engage with something like this right now...<br /> <br /> {looks out the window at current state of the world}<br /> <br /> Yeaaahh, definitely still need to be focusing on the positive.<br /> <br /> Perhaps when this is all behind us.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T18:19:03-07:00","id":1377617,"title":"","content":"Damn, was hoping you\u2019d be the one to talk about the Texas Sharpshooter elements present in arguments for \u201cmaking biological sense.\u201d I certainly haven\u2019t got it in me. :-/","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 03-31-2020, 09:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T19:50:33-07:00","id":1377631,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sith_Happens</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377602#post1377602\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The hypothetical custom Sunder was commenting on is plainly a twist on the \u201cExpendable sex\u201d concept so it\u2019s perfectly reasonable to speculate on how the same attitude might affect marriage trends. Didn\u2019t need to go on at nearly such length about it though, especially when just as important is the part where there\u2019ll be more older men and younger women to go around in the first place thanks to their not being the ones dying on the front lines (a literal process of elimination, one might call it).</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think I was describing a custom, but the sexually dimorphic nature of the human species, which constraints and informs all possible cultures.<br /> <br /> Without the intervention of magic, can you envision a culture in which young men are more valuable than young women? Or one where women are encouraged to delay marrying until they would experience pregnancy complications, and then marry younger men who cannot provide as well as older men?<br /> <br /> The matriarchy of the Realm doesn't ignore the realities that men and women are different.<br /> <br /> They recognize that men are more aggressive and risk-prone than women. They simply frame it differently, through the negative stereotypes that men are too hotheaded and impulsive to be trusted with the highest levels of power and authority.<br /> <br /> They also don't ignore the ease with which men can impregnate and never know it, or else deny involvement, while women can be absolutely certain they are someone's mother. The Realm clearly acknowledge this with its practice regarding Lost Eggs; isn't that only Exalted women can bring their children into their House, while Lost Eggs sired by Exalted men are denied that recognition?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T21:18:31-07:00","id":1377645,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377631#post1377631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think I was describing a custom, but the sexually dimorphic nature of the human species, which constraints and informs all possible cultures.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well you could have fooled me because it sure looked like you started out speaking specifically in reference to the thing you quoted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Without the intervention of magic </div> </div> </div>The intervention of magic is taken for granted in Creation but okay I'll bite.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> can you envision a culture in which young men are more valuable than young women? </div> </div> </div>For most kinds of value there's an ongoing effort to let us stop living in one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or one where women are encouraged to delay marrying until they would experience pregnancy complications, and then marry younger men who cannot provide as well as older men? </div> </div> </div>That's easy, they just have to value population growth little enough and encourage men to do the gold-digging.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They recognize that men are more aggressive and risk-prone than women. They simply frame it differently, through the negative stereotypes that men are too hotheaded and impulsive to be trusted with the highest levels of power and authority. </div> </div> </div>The writers' dirty secret is that this is just a few synonyms away from being a common stereotype of women IRL.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They also don't ignore the ease with which men can impregnate and never know it, or else deny involvement, while women can be absolutely certain they are someone's mother. The Realm clearly acknowledge this with its practice regarding Lost Eggs; isn't that only Exalted women can bring their children into their House, while Lost Eggs sired by Exalted men are denied that recognition? </div> </div> </div>It'd be cool if matrilinearity were more common, yes. It's just so much simpler.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","user":"Sith_Happens","date":". 04-01-2020, 12:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T21:43:54-07:00","id":1377650,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377631#post1377631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think I was describing a custom, but the sexually dimorphic nature of the human species, which constraints and informs all possible cultures.<br /> <br /> Without the intervention of magic, can you envision a culture in which young men are more valuable than young women?</div> </div> </div> </div>Check out how infanticide rates between the sexes of children shake out through history. If that's any indication, either massive amounts of slain newborn boys went missing, or lots of families didn't want a girl, because that means a dowry or something of the sort. Which, as you will note, is a purely cultural construct.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or one where women are encouraged to delay marrying until they would experience pregnancy complications, and then marry younger men who cannot provide as well as older men?<br /> <br /> The matriarchy of the Realm doesn't ignore the realities that men and women are different.<br /> <br /> They recognize that men are more aggressive and risk-prone than women. They simply frame it differently, through the negative stereotypes that men are too hotheaded and impulsive to be trusted with the highest levels of power and authority.<br /> <br /> They also don't ignore the ease with which men can impregnate and never know it, or else deny involvement, while women can be absolutely certain they are someone's mother. The Realm clearly acknowledge this with its practice regarding Lost Eggs; isn't that only Exalted women can bring their children into their House, while Lost Eggs sired by Exalted men are denied that recognition? </div> </div> </div>You've vastly overstepped the bounds of biological influence here. Human sexual dimorphism is tiny compared to other hominids, and in any case, this is a group difference. Variance between individuals is more substantial and noticeable than variance between group populations. What's more, testosterone isn't some kind of psycho serum, and a lot of risktaking behavior we see in modern dudes has its basis in social conditioning. Cultural bias is also ubiquitous in human observations, including studies, so trying to separate the nature from the nurture is not readily or easily accomplished. I do believe that this has come up before on the forum, but that might have been a few years ago, so I suppose a reminder might be in order.<br /> <br /> Note also that, as far as contraceptives go in Exalted, it's four times as expensive to cover a man for a month than it is to cover a woman for the same time, assuming use of maiden tea. That's also going to fuel a misconception, as the theoretical financial burden might cause a number of men to try alternative methods to conserve resources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T22:28:33-07:00","id":1377656,"title":"","content":"Wait I don't remember anything indicating Ligier was upset because Dancing was feminine. It seemed more he was facepalm because it was unseemly to dance without a care in front of the Rabble inside your own inverted Tortured Corpse Prison.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T00:14:42-07:00","id":1377676,"title":"","content":"Pretty much, yeah. Others made comments of something to that effect in the first page (though they did so by evoking the image of the Queen of England slam-dancing).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T00:49:55-07:00","id":1377680,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377650#post1377650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Cultural bias is also ubiquitous in human observations, including studies, so trying to separate the nature from the nurture is not readily or easily accomplished. I do believe that this has come up before on the forum, but that might have been a few years ago, so I suppose a reminder might be in order.</div> </div> </div> </div>The difference would have to be enormous to warrant women not going to war anyway. You could run an ANOVA test and prove with a 95% confidence that sex is a significant factor in combat effectiveness and you'd still bring every women you could to fight. How many commanders would literally give their right arm to double their forces, even if the new soldiers were even half as effective as their current ones. If nothing else you could have the weaker and less motivated troops stay at the back and act as intimidation fuel, which is how half the battles got won anyway.<br /> <br /> Regardless of how bad an idea it is to heap the majority of the responsibility to fight on men is, it's something that's happened almost everywhere. There are some pretty notable exceptions, but one of the things that makes them notable is their exception. Creation doesn't exactly flip this, since you're really not going to hear &quot;Did you hear about that regiment from Chiaroscuro? The Bearded Devils? They're all <i>male. </i>I hear that they're even more vicious and bloodthirsty than the rest of the army. How crazy is that?&quot;<br /> <br /> Instead that exists at the officer level, but the rank and file is closer to 50/50. Which might make Creation's armies vastly larger than you'd expect compared to Earth armies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T07:37:53-07:00","id":1377729,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377680#post1377680\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which might make Creation's armies vastly larger than you'd expect compared to Earth armies.</div> </div> </div> </div>Armies can be no bigger than you can afford.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T07:51:38-07:00","id":1377731,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377656#post1377656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wait I don't remember anything indicating Ligier was upset because Dancing was feminine. It seemed more he was facepalm because it was unseemly to dance without a care in front of the Rabble inside your own inverted Tortured Corpse Prison.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Malfeas is also a brass-skinned man known for ecstasies and viciousness. He dances straight through the centers of his streets and performs great, tragic dramas in his latest square. Still, he always employs some restraint, for he prefers to not feel the disdain of noble Ligier.&quot;<br /> <br /> As it first appears in Roll of Glorious Divinities, it's a single throwaway line with no qualifiers or elaboration. Any further significance comes from the reader.<br /> <br /> I've grown to dislike it, but if I'm being honest I don't think it's a bad way to present such a thing, and one that's really kind of inviting for the reader.<br /> <br /> I guess I've taken more issue with how it played out in forum culture and how that contributed to crowding out a lot of what else made the demon interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T07:54:56-07:00","id":1377732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sith_Happens</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377645#post1377645\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well you could have fooled me because it sure looked like you started out speaking specifically in reference to the thing you quoted.</div> </div> </div> </div>I did, yes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The intervention of magic is taken for granted in Creation but okay I'll bite. </div> </div> </div>Magic exists in Creation, but is always exceptional. Even if every village in Creation has access to magic or is forced to deal with malevolent magic, it's all highly variable.<br /> <br /> And it's all an external factor which doesn't change the inherent internal nature of humanity. Unless it actually, actively changes that nature at some point, as by transforming the humans into horsefish people where the men carry the babies until birth.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For most kinds of value there's an ongoing effort to let us stop living in one. </div> </div> </div>What kind of answer is that?<br /> <br /> Even here in America, the richest country in the world, when a young woman is using one of those Hook Up apps, who do you think she accepts invitations from? High school boys or college boys? College boys are more likely to have their own place, cars, and disposable money. But she also gets invitations from college graduates.<br /> <br /> When women have access to the entire market of men, and get invitations from all of those men at once, why would she settle for less? Why SHOULD she settle for less?<br /> <br /> Why do rich and powerful men, who have access to the entire market of women, favor younger women rather than women their own age?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's easy, they just have to value population growth little enough </div> </div> </div>We can definitely find examples of efforts to reduce population growth in the real world. Examples SO successful that multiple nations are now panicked, and trying to fix the damage before they disappear entirely from a lack of population replacement.<br /> <br /> Japan still hasn't figured out how and they're still in Panda-levels of jeopardy.<br /> <br /> China absolutely screwed itself up with the One Child Policy, which resulted in the Girl Genocide. Not because young women lost their value in the mating competition, but because parents have different values than bachelors, and in China, parents rely on sons growing up into men who can support them in their old age. There's a growing industry for female dolls, because all of these surviving young men are so desperate.<br /> <br /> Even in <i>Exalted</i>, the Scarlet Empress explicitly designed Dynastic society to frustrate and retard the growth of the Dragon-Blooded population.<br /> <br /> But see, in every example, the leaders in charge were specifically trying to fight human nature. They all succeeded, but they all needed to actually try. And at least in the real world, the consequences were always worse than the situation they were trying to avoid in the first place!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and encourage men to do the gold-digging. </div> </div> </div>Examples?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The writers' dirty secret is that this is just a few synonyms away from being a common stereotype of women IRL. </div> </div> </div>Also of slaves. All slaves, all across the world, which historically included a lot of your own people who fell to far into debt or who lost their rights as some kind of punishment.<br /> <br /> And the people of a nation you're oppressing. Or at war with.<br /> <br /> When you want to dehumanize someone, you go for the quality that sets humans apart from animals: The ability to self-control.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It'd be cool if matrilinearity were more common, yes. It's just so much simpler. </div> </div> </div>It's simpler, but it removes men from responsibility. It removes their stake in the equation.<br /> <br /> If you make a man responsible and rewarded for his place in genealogy, it gives him a reason to care about it. It's his record to screw up, to be ashamed of or at peace with.<br /> <br /> Enough men already make a mess of things in patrilineal systems. Do you imagine those men would have behaved <i>better</i> in a purely matrilineal system?<br /> <br /> Don't women already have enough responsibility, given how much harder a pregnancy is to escape for them?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":636,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","user":"Sunder the Gold","date":". 04-01-2020, 10:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T08:04:21-07:00","id":1377736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377729#post1377729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Armies can be no bigger than you can afford.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a key point.<br /> <br /> With a few exceptions, the issue for most states hasn't generally (except after really brutal) been that they don't have enough people to be soldiers. It's that they don't have enough money and food to support such an army. If we look at medieval armies (a time whose governments were extremely focused around the military), the number of soldiers is actually <i>tiny</i> compared to the population. They could have levied more troops... but they wouldn't have enough armour, weapons, and most importantly food for them.<br /> Saying &quot;women can join the army&quot; might have let them levy slightly more people... but then those women aren't farming, repairing soldiers' clothes, farming, repairing their boots, farming, etc, etc.<br /> Actually, most men weren't levied either, because then there's not enough farmers. (Also, bringing a horde of unskilled, untrained peasants isn't very effective compared to a smaller number of skilled professionals.)<br /> <br /> The only time medieval leaders tended to get as many people as possible to fight was when their city was being stormed by an attacking army. And that was one of the small number of situations in which women actually <i>did</i> fight.<br /> <br /> So Creation's more egalitarian armies might be slightly bigger, and a bit more effective, but there's still the key issue of what a leader can <i>afford </i>to put into the field.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T08:06:57-07:00","id":1377737,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377731#post1377731\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I guess I've taken more issue with how it played out in forum culture and how that contributed to crowding out a lot of what else made the demon interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like many things!<br /> <br /> I liked it as showing how different parts of Malfeas have differing values, but are also still Malfeas. But it possibly could have been explained a bit better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T09:16:24-07:00","id":1377751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377650#post1377650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Check out how infanticide rates between the sexes of children shake out through history. If that's any indication, either massive amounts of slain newborn boys went missing, or lots of families didn't want a girl, because that means a dowry or something of the sort. Which, as you will note, is a purely cultural construct.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a valid point.<br /> <br /> As I covered after your post, in the previous one where I mentioned China's disastrous and inhumane One Child Policy, an individual actually has multiple &quot;societal values&quot;.<br /> <br /> Parents and bachelors have different priorities, so when parents are pressured to keep only one child, they are more likely to want a son who can grow up to become a man that provides for them in their old age.<br /> <br /> For the few young women who survive, that doesn't actually change the equation much for them. When they reach marriageable age, they simply have more choices. The previous generation may have valued them less, but the current generation will value them MORE because there are so few of them available.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Human sexual dimorphism is tiny compared to other hominids </div> </div> </div>And yet, the vast majority of firefighters, trash collectors, sewer maintenance workers, and football players are men. Our sports competitions are strictly segregated by gender.<br /> <br /> Men are more often color-blind, while women generally have better color-vision and a better sense of smell. The better to notice that something is wrong with food before giving it to children, or that a child has turned an unhealthy shade before the child is even aware of feeling sick.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T09:29:57-07:00","id":1377754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377729#post1377729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Armies can be no bigger than you can afford.</div> </div> </div> </div>Unless your entire able-bodied population is your army, as with horse-tribes like the Scythians. I believe I read that all women had to know how to shoot bows on horseback.<br /> <br /> I know I read one account where all of the men had left the tribe to go to conquer this one city, and they stayed there so long (confident that their women were capable of taking care of themselves in their absence) that eventually the women sent a letter threatening to decamp and find other husbands.<br /> <br /> I think there was also another legendary account of a woman who led the tribe during a war with some empire, and she cut off the emperor's head and drank wine from his skull in vengeance for her dead sons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T09:39:50-07:00","id":1377758,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377754#post1377754\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unless your entire able-bodied population is your army, as with horse-tribes like the Scythians. I believe I read that all women had to know how to shoot bows on horseback.</div> </div> </div> </div>True. Unusual, but certainly with the European steppe hordes their usual way of life happened to make them okay soldiers, allowing them to raise massive armies.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T10:13:22-07:00","id":1377761,"title":"","content":"I've heard some suggestions that sort of a myth, and that the likes of the Scythians and Mongols probably really had a substantial &quot;commoner&quot; population which did not fight and generally provided logistical support, than is commonly thought.<br /> <br /> The Mongols' could mobilize more population than many comparable agricultural populations though. IMO that really does have more to do with the facts of life before the state though, really. <br /> Before you have big states in play, necessarily every young man (and perhaps young women in some instances), would have to have some degree of &quot;military&quot; experience, to defend their own property. What else can they do? There's no army, and there's no police to protect them against theft.<br /> <br /> Once big states are in play, the army and police (or watch or what have you), enforces the law and individuals no longer have to be trained. <br /> <br /> As well as this Malthusian tendencies mean that any spare capacity to mobilize tends to be used up. Primitive farmers tend to not indulge in too much farming effort, as they can't afford to, so pursue strategies that limit their population size but leave them plenty of time to fight. There's slack. But once peace is assured, people can afford to engage in a lot more high effort and committed agriculture, but end up with very little room to deviate spending a lot of time in this, without much time to train, and less time to go away and fight.<br /> <br /> So once all that's used up, groups like the Mongols often found it was actually more of a pretty even fight with very large agricultural empires than you might think, particularly if they were willing to recruit. Head-for-head, they could built pretty competitive sized armies, if they could unite.<br /> <br /> Pastoralist invading armies like the Mongol Hordes are, IMO, more like &quot;invaders from the past, with modern technology&quot;, in their context.<br /> <br /> From what I understand, there were some states who were &quot;civilized&quot; and still went really hard for mobilizing big armies - the Roman Republic, the Warring States (particularly Qin), maybe the Greek city states. But they had to be pushed to do so by a pretty tense local geopolitical situation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T13:35:14-07:00","id":1377792,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377751#post1377751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a valid point.<br /> <br /> As I covered after your post, in the previous one where I mentioned China's disastrous and inhumane One Child Policy, an individual actually has multiple &quot;societal values&quot;.<br /> <br /> Parents and bachelors have different priorities, so when parents are pressured to keep only one child, they are more likely to want a son who can grow up to become a man that provides for them in their old age.<br /> <br /> For the few young women who survive, that doesn't actually change the equation much for them. When they reach marriageable age, they simply have more choices. The previous generation may have valued them less, but the current generation will value them MORE because there are so few of them available.</div> </div> </div> </div>This wasn't about modern China, though, but Europe until rather recently.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And yet, the vast majority of firefighters, trash collectors, sewer maintenance workers, and football players are men. Our sports competitions are strictly segregated by gender. </div> </div> </div>For purely cultural reasons. In the case of sports, this has resulted in particularly bizarre choices like banning women with high natural testosterone counts from running. In any case, you're continually asserting examples from the bowels of the bias. Take the US, for example; does it strike you as particularly welcoming of women bulking up and weight-lifting?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Men are more often color-blind, while women generally have better color-vision and a better sense of smell. The better to notice that something is wrong with food before giving it to children, or that a child has turned an unhealthy shade before the child is even aware of feeling sick. </div> </div> </div>X-linked traits are going to show up in more men than women on account of how cis men have only one X chromosome and hence only one allele is going to produce the trait on a macro level, where cis women would have to inherit the allele from both parents. That seems to support your case for color vision, but what about hemophilia? Muscular dystrophy? How would it even begin to explain androgen insensitivity? These things are accidents of molecular biology, not indelible proscriptions for biologically-encoded social roles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T14:02:40-07:00","id":1377795,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377792#post1377792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This wasn't about modern China, though, but Europe until rather recently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Please elaborate.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For purely cultural reasons. </div> </div> </div>Firefighters in particular need to physically strong enough to bodily lift the deadweight of your average male citizen in order to carry them out of burning buildings. That's not cultural. Culture would be lowering that standard so that more people could qualify.<br /> <br /> I don't recall any stories of women applying for work as trash collectors and being turned away at the door. Can you share any?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In the case of sports, this has resulted in particularly bizarre choices like banning women with high natural testosterone counts from running. </div> </div> </div>Can you point me to that news article?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In any case, you're continually asserting examples from the bowels of the bias. </div> </div> </div>You're not citing anything yourself, so you're also talking from your bias.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Take the US, for example; does it strike you as particularly welcoming of women bulking up and weight-lifting? </div> </div> </div>No one's made that illegal, so yes.<br /> <br /> Are the women doing it to make themselves sexually appealing? I know there are men who find physically powerful women a turn-on.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> X-linked traits are going to show up in more men than women on account of how cis men have only one X chromosome and hence only one allele is going to produce the trait on a macro level, where cis women would have to inherit the allele from both parents. That seems to support your case for color vision, but what about hemophilia? Muscular dystrophy? How would it even begin to explain androgen insensitivity? </div> </div> </div>Outside of my experience; you'd have to educate me.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> These things are accidents of molecular biology, not indelible proscriptions for biologically-encoded social roles. </div> </div> </div>Social roles? When did I argue for something as strict as roles?<br /> <br /> I was talking about trends. Collectives are made of individuals; individuals fall within spectrums. Individuals can be exceptional.<br /> <br /> I've owned four cats in my life. All female. Three were Calicos. All of them were unique from each other in their own ways. They shared a common nature, but they had different talents, different personalities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-01T15:13:24-07:00","id":1377805,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377795#post1377795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Please elaborate.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">What do you think the end result of having to pony up money to get a kid married is? See also, page 3 </span></span></span></div><a href=\"https://books.google.com/books?hl=en&amp;lr=&amp;id=WYU8Q5z0rO4C&amp;oi=fnd&amp;pg=PP1&amp;dq=infanticide+in+pre-modern+europe&amp;ots=fNxrIeRXqg&amp;sig=TgYq3ADvntwM_CwoAAOLzV_8RtM#v=onepage&amp;q=infanticide%20in%20pre-modern%20europe&amp;f=false\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">. The rest of the book can go into further detail.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Firefighters in particular need to physically strong enough to bodily lift the deadweight of your average male citizen in order to carry them out of burning buildings. That's not cultural. Culture would be lowering that standard so that more people could qualify.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Culture would be in not throwing aspersions at women who engage in bodybuilding and other strength-building exercise or making the overall locker room atmosphere what it is. Because if you make the training environment hell, the end result is that fewer people think it's worth the shit that's piled on them. Some literature that goes into it </span></span></span></div><a href=\"http://openaccess.bilgi.edu.tr:8080/xmlui/handle/11411/1650\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I don't recall any stories of women applying for work as trash collectors and being turned away at the door. Can you share any?</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The burden of proof for the assertion that you originally made is on you, there.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Can you point me to that news article?</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Google for Caster Semenya. There should be a long-reaching chain of articles that results.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">You're not citing anything yourself, so you're also talking from your bias.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My emotional stake in this is that I get really angry when people don't consider their own influence in trying to tow the line. Which you are presently doing. My bias is not equivalent to yours in this conversation, and remains even when I cite literature.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">No one's made that illegal, so yes.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">That's an extremely disingenous response. Do you not know the difference between de facto and de jure discrimination?</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Are the women doing it to make themselves sexually appealing? I know there are men who find physically powerful women a turn-on.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">That doesn't seem to be a mainstream thing, else it would be encouraged in common practice, and men wouldn't be defensive and snippy about women practicing sports. Which, as noted in academic documentation above, has been the case for a long time.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Outside of my experience; you'd have to educate me.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The answer is that it doesn't.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Social roles? When did I argue for something as strict as roles?</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I was talking about trends. Collectives are made of individuals; individuals fall within spectrums. Individuals can be exceptional.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I've owned four cats in my life. All female. Three were Calicos. All of them were unique from each other in their own ways. They shared a common nature, but they had different talents, different personalities.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Find out what the actual trends are first. This includes the cultural ones. Note that because of how intimately culture is tied into human activities, you have to assume that there is a major cultural influence that can override anything that's allegedly apparent from &quot;biological reasons&quot;, because environmental factors affect biology.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T00:06:16-07:00","id":1377898,"title":"","content":"You set yourself on a much more complicated corner with your &quot;purely cultural reasons&quot; affirmation than you think. <br /> For starters, &quot;culture&quot; is often an ill defined, fuzzy term, especially over a casual conversation like this one. It's very hard to make predictive, reproducible, falseable arguments over that. <br /> That affirmation also demands proof in the form of linking <i>every behavioral phenomenon</i><i> you can see</i> to it. It's such a demanding statement that it can break with any single study that spots behavioral differences tied to non-cultural elements.<br /> Symmetrically, someone affirming everything is purely instinct is also going nowhere, as they have to prove that two people with the same background can't come with different solutions given the same circumstances (a bit like the deterministic universe question). It also breaks the moment two similar people take visibly different courses of action.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> There are much, much simpler and useful affirmations that you can make: Humans are animals. Social animals, which is a whole category of animals based on how they behave and various cognitive traits that help. Humans develop highly complex societal behaviors with trends that we often label &quot;culture&quot;.<br /> <br /> Both exist, and both can play into each other. These things that break the two arguments at the start? They Prove this one. <br /> <br /> <br /> A very basic example of &quot;not purely cultural trait&quot; is one of the more common forms of mimicry. When everyone is behaving in a certain way almost every newcomer also adopts that behavior, without knowing why it happens, even if it's nonsensical.<br /> This necessity to blend in propagates through generations and is visible in every single culture (it's also seen on other social animals, humans do not monopolize that). If it was &quot;purely instinct&quot; different cultures would have negligible effects on this behavior. If it was &quot;Purely cultural&quot; it'd not happen in societies that disavow herding like that.<br /> Instead what you see is that it happens &quot;more often&quot; or &quot;less often&quot; according to how demanding of conformity those societies are, and is more or less sensible to rebel individuals.<br /> <br /> <br /> Yet another is sexual selection. You can easily affirm people &quot;have standards&quot;, with some people being very &quot;high demand&quot; and some &quot;dejected&quot;. <i>You </i>will spot that behavior in every society. In every <i>animal</i>. But you can also easily show that those standards are tied to cultural perceptions. One does not exist without the other.<br /> <br /> <br /> Hells, even something like &quot;empathy&quot; includes traits that go away when the person is taking <i>painkillers.</i> Brain chemistry is not cultural. But how much you are expected to exercise empathy is very much cultural, and produces tangible results in these measurements.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T02:53:52-07:00","id":1377916,"title":"","content":"Darn it, I might have known that piece of information I came across about how there's growing paleontological/archaeological evidence that differences in the upper threshold of strength between men and women were a lot narrower in something like the Ice Age forager days (when several aspects of lifestyle, diet and culture would have been different and perhaps less concerned with segregation) was but an omen of horrors to come. <br /> <br /> I did not save the links, and now worry I may never find them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T03:14:10-07:00","id":1377919,"title":"","content":"Pity you've lost them, because that'd be interesting, both generally and vis-a-vis the game I run, where an Ice Age is ending.<br /> <br /> So anyway, rather than this dumpster-fire argument where people are focusing again and again on individual sentences going in all sorts of directions and not making real overall points...<br /> How about that OP? What interesting things have you done with gender roles in your games? I mentioned for example, complex gender roles regarding inheritance and monarchy in the game I used to run.<br /> What about your games?<br /> (Is this actually possible to discuss without getting into dumpster-fire arguments? I'm... not sure, honestly. But let's give it a try? I suggest sticking to short, 3-4 paragraph answers, and focusing on overall points rather than minutiae.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T04:08:02-07:00","id":1377921,"title":"","content":"Re, strength over time, there's a typical trend of reduced strength through time (e.g. <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/321371514_Prehistoric_women&#39;s_manual_labor_exceeded_that_of_athletes_through_the_first_5500_years_of_farming_in_Central_Europe\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.researchgate.net/publica...Central_Europe</a>). <br /> <br /> But out of all the studies (usually based on Ruff's datasets) that I'm aware of, I can't remember that there are any which really suggest reduced much dimorphism (there may be small differences, but it's more like both sexes were +/-, not so much that ancient people were compressed towards less strength sexual dimorphism). <br /> <br /> <br /> I think I've seen some Upper Palaeolithic ones that suggest modestly increased dimorphism (probably because suboptimal nutrition in subsequent Mesolithic to early modern periods compresses men more, because they have higher genetic &quot;growth potential&quot;). But generally it seems like all populations pretty much preserve similar dimorphism in height and strength. (People across bio sex groups, within single population are doing the same kinds of physical intensity, same kind of nutrition, same underlying genetic pools, so very little room to diverge).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T04:19:46-07:00","id":1377923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377921#post1377921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">modestly increased dimorphism (probably because suboptimal nutrition... compresses men more</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, I'm not quite following you there. If suboptimal nutrition reduced the taller gender's height more, wouldn't there be less dimorphism?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T09:07:33-07:00","id":1377959,"title":"","content":"Apologies, increased dimorphism in Upper Paleolithic *relative to* Mesolithic-&gt;early modern periods (when more suboptimal nutrition obtained), because better nutrition then (it seems like, from height). Less so that Upper Paleolithic -&gt; increase dimorphism relative to today.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T09:56:27-07:00","id":1377974,"title":"","content":"So, the significance of the relationship between the Chante-Sa and the Three Mothers as an example of divinity as it might pertain to a given culture's idea of gender roles. <br /> <br /> I've got a few ideas of the nuances of the nature of the goddesses and their relationships with one another and their worshippers, but for the sake of simplicity I'll just present the subject in terms that take them being characterized as maternal deities at face value.<br /> <br /> So we've got a society that does seem to have some values for gender roles related to maternity, albeit with a few details mixed in that might make them unfamiliar to us. For instance I think the Flower Band by way of its connection to Chuwe'Inan has ideas that associate femininity with beauty, but in the context of an environment in which beauty is also recognised as a flamboyant declaration of impending danger.<br /> <br /> I think it does leave an open question of whether or not these are cultural values developed from an example set by proximity to those goddesses, if not actively promoted by them, or things that they already adhered to which is the basis for them adopting these particular goddesses over any others.<br /> <br /> And that itself seems to play into how they've formed a relationship with Ma-Ha-Suchi. The role of doula might not necessarily be traditionally feminine, but in an instance like this I don't think it's a stretch to assume it is, so I would think that them perceiving the Lunar's magic transforming people into beastfolk in that role both takes the form of adopting that practice into a particular kind of privileged traditional gender role, while also being an instance of a society loosening or subverting their own tradition by describing Ma-Ha-Suchi as having that role.<br /> <br /> I'd view it as such a subversion because their perception of Ma-Ha-Suchi seems to otherwise be focused on categorizing him as a man by way of him being the &quot;Undying Uncle&quot;. I think that itself connotes the society's standards for gender roles, because he seems to hold status almost equal to a Mother but is not given that title, but nor is he given its direct male counterpart, but a more tangential one. One might suggest that Ma-Ha-Suchi presenting as male actually forms a check on the status he can hold among the Chante-Sa, although at the same time his being a Lunar (and having their associated complex relationship with gender) is a basis by which they can provide for him inhabiting an otherwise feminine role.<br /> <br /> I don't really have a wider point here so much as a bunch of observations of a particular example that I think are interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-02T13:20:02-07:00","id":1378025,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Synapse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377898#post1377898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You set yourself on a much more complicated corner with your &quot;purely cultural reasons&quot; affirmation than you think.</div> </div> </div> </div></span></span></span></div> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Synapse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1377898#post1377898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">For starters, &quot;culture&quot; is often an ill defined, fuzzy term, especially over a casual conversation like this one. It's very hard to make predictive, reproducible, falseable arguments over that.</span></span></span></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">That affirmation also demands proof in the form of linking <i>every behavioral phenomenon</i><i> you can see</i> to it. It's such a demanding statement that it can break with any single study that spots behavioral differences tied to non-cultural elements.</span></span></span></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Symmetrically, someone affirming everything is purely instinct is also going nowhere, as they have to prove that two people with the same background can't come with different solutions given the same circumstances (a bit like the deterministic universe question). It also breaks the moment two similar people take visibly different courses of action.</span></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">There are much, much simpler and useful affirmations that you can make: Humans are animals. Social animals, which is a whole category of animals based on how they behave and various cognitive traits that help. Humans develop highly complex societal behaviors with trends that we often label &quot;culture&quot;.</span></span></span></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Both exist, and both can play into each other. These things that break the two arguments at the start? They Prove this one.</span></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">A very basic example of &quot;not purely cultural trait&quot; is one of the more common forms of mimicry. When everyone is behaving in a certain way almost every newcomer also adopts that behavior, without knowing why it happens, even if it's nonsensical.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Mimicry seems to be something that allows for the development of cultural information in the first place. On the flip side, some social animals will also dispense harsh punishments for non-conforming conspecifics.</span></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">This necessity to blend in propagates through generations and is visible in every single culture (it's also seen on other social animals, humans do not monopolize that). If it was &quot;purely instinct&quot; different cultures would have negligible effects on this behavior. If it was &quot;Purely cultural&quot; it'd not happen in societies that disavow herding like that.</span></span></span></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Instead what you see is that it happens &quot;more often&quot; or &quot;less often&quot; according to how demanding of conformity those societies are, and is more or less sensible to rebel individuals.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Well, I'm never going to use the term &quot;instinct&quot;, much less a pure one, so there's that. We also know that nature vs. nurture is a phony debate, and that it's often complicated to separate one from the other. Highly conserved capabilities shared broadly among animals are probably reflective of biological traits.</span></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Yet another is sexual selection. You can easily affirm people &quot;have standards&quot;, with some people being very &quot;high demand&quot; and some &quot;dejected&quot;. <i>You </i>will spot that behavior in every society. In every <i>animal</i>. But you can also easily show that those standards are tied to cultural perceptions. One does not exist without the other.</span></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Hells, even something like &quot;empathy&quot; includes traits that go away when the person is taking <i>painkillers.</i> Brain chemistry is not cultural. But how much you are expected to exercise empathy is very much cultural, and produces tangible results in these measurements.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Anxiety controllers, actually, though short-circuiting the comprehension of pain might do the trick, too.</span></span></span></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">So, as discusses above by other posters, if increased resources are required to &quot;make the most of male potential&quot;, control over resources is a logical cultural step required to meet that physiological requirement. It's established among chimpanzees, one of humanity's closest evolutionary cousins, that male chimps are pretty aggressive in leading hunts on monkeys to acquire meat, and also tend to be notably larger and stronger than female chimps, by a margin a lot greater than human sex differences. They also tend to carefully control who gets meat when they end up killing a monkey; this is often done to cement alliances with other males, and thereby gain leadership positions or concessions from a leader.</span></span></span></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">You can see a fair bit of humanity in that, being built in such a similar manner. One could also see how resource monopolization might end up setting a long-lasting bias up on the basis of male vs. female strength due to male humans taking control over kills and distribution of food to exacerbate what would otherwise be a much more modest difference - not only do the males get more of the high protein source, the females get less of it, and if cultural standards shift away from catching smaller animals in nets and snares for both (as was the case for most of human existence), that would reasonably result in a substantial disruption to readability of biology due to culture. If the groups in charge then also restrict access to exercise, that's another hit (that can come from pressure on both sides; kyriarchy and all). Eventually, it honestly seems like men are naturally stronger, even though these are due overwhelmingly to culturally-wrangled advantages. The biological basis then becomes very difficult to measure genuinely. That's where we are. That's our problem.<br /> <br /> Edit Addendum: Control over calorie distribution without being physically powerful already can be accomplished through improved stone points, walking after prey until they becoming exhausted, or as a variation, walking after prey until it falls off of a cliff. Also, fat content is going to be as important as protein, since a skeletal muscle hypertrophy requires a great deal of energy for maintenance. Bones and meat also contain micronutrients to this effect; calcium is necessary to get muscle to move and build up stronger bones, of course, but improving zinc content in diet, such as from red meat or lentils, can ease healing, support eyesight, smell, and taste, and improve fertility, male fertility in particular.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 04-02-2020, 05:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-03T01:52:39-07:00","id":1378210,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1378025#post1378025\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The biological basis then becomes very difficult to measure genuinely. That's where we are. That's our problem.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div> This ignores, however, that malnutrition has long become a problem beneath the average person. Both males and females today are consistently taller, stronger and even a bit smarter because we faced very little impediments to our formative years compared to those even just a century ago. <br /> <br /> This added difficulty, too, is an added difficulty, not an impossibility. Scientists are better at this than you are giving them credit.<br /> <br /> For example, testosterone is a known facilitator of building muscle mass, and it's consistently more prevalent in males. Another example of how this has non-cultural factors is that you will find those differences in both &quot;common folk&quot; and high performance athletes, which have distinct societal pressures and deterrents from their peers and associates.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-03T12:26:44-07:00","id":1378317,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Synapse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369156-scary-scary-gender-roles-are-scary-~?p=1378210#post1378210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> This ignores, however, that malnutrition has long become a problem beneath the average person. Both males and females today are consistently taller, stronger and even a bit smarter because we faced very little impediments to our formative years compared to those even just a century ago.<br /> <br /> This added difficulty, too, is an added difficulty, not an impossibility. Scientists are better at this than you are giving them credit.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are still major widespread social impediments that are putting their hands on the scale, with whole industries built up along gendered lines that don't reflect a &quot;natural tendency&quot; toward anything, instead being representative of marketing influences in culture. Again, the pressures in activity and nutrition consumption are not equal; find me cultures that promote undiluted machismo in women and relegate men to inactivity for aesthetic reasons, with no influence from an observer that would disrupt the culture in question, and you'll find an interesting lead. Until then, you're just using uncertain estimators.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For example, testosterone is a known facilitator of building muscle mass, and it's consistently more prevalent in males. Another example of how this has non-cultural factors is that you will find those differences in both &quot;common folk&quot; and high performance athletes, which have distinct societal pressures and deterrents from their peers and associates. </div> </div> </div>Testosterone can also vary wildly in concentration from day to day, and there isn't really a legitimate baseline established for it. In measuring hormone responses, you would also need to find the genes for the receptors to said hormones and measure the activity and responsiveness of those, because if you don't have receptors to accommodate a high concentration, that concentration can become a moot point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-03T13:39:13-07:00","id":1378332,"title":"","content":"Re, upthread idea, in terms of protein, I would guess that, specifically, differential access probably didn't matter *too* much. My reasoning there is that the limit of biological protein you might need, may not be that high to develop strength, and it may be that most societies get there in terms of pure protein amount for most members (or if they're not they're generally not for both men and women).<br /> <br /> Sure, yes, we can talk about apes that fairly rarely eat animal food sources and use them in kind of a &quot;luxury goods&quot;-alliance &quot;show-off&quot; kind of way, but it's worth bringing us back to measured human HG societies who seem to get about at least 45% of calories from hunted and fished sources, varying up to 94%, mostly varying by latitude (<a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/ajcn/article/71/3/682/4729121\" target=\"_blank\">https://academic.oup.com/ajcn/article/71/3/682/4729121</a>). (It may have been more for Upper Paleolithic hunters who concentrated on large prey, before humans kind of exceeded our hunting capacity and those habitats where large prey were more concentrated and an open landscape meant they were easier to catch, and then had to go for smaller prey, more plants, etc - the &quot;Broad Spectrum Revolution&quot;).<br /> <br /> That's probably because it's a pretty efficient way to get calories (with a lot of that actually being animal fat), and the actual requirement of protein probably far lower (and if you eat too much protein as a share of subsistence you may get ill, as per above, so hunters have to preferentially target fat-bearing animals to some degree).<br /> <br /> Where you have a lot of malnutrition through human history and pre-history and failing to thrive, that's possibly more because people just aren't getting enough calories period, rather than a lack specifically of protein content.<br /> <br /> So I don't know. In terms of allocation of protein, I think probably not. May work better as a theory that men tend to get more calories because they do a higher proportion of the calorie gathering work in societies, where women seem to tend to have more of their time used for childcare, in-camp work (tailoring clothing, pottery, etc), caring for elderly and injured group members etc (stuff that's skilled and vital for the group but not really calorie gathering).<br /> <br /> But then you'd actually have to test how food seemed to be allocated among groups and the likely effect size of that. Like, do you actually have a increased sexual inequality of malnutrition signatures in places where male share of subsistence tends to be somewhat larger (usually North Eurasian / North American very cold places where hunting and fishing is almost the only thing you can do for subsistence)?<br /> <br /> (One of the ways this can also be checked among ancient populations is by looking at the nitrogen isotope ratios is bone, and from that you can determine if there was a dietary difference between males and females in animal, fished, plant foods, and also they can look at indicators of nutritional stress like cribia orbitale, enamel hypoplasia and the like and see if there are any systematic differences between male and female samples.)<br /> <br /> Another last thing to add, getting back to the alliance thing, I think for the most part, the ethnographic stuff (from what I've heard secondhand) tends to support that hunting activity is mostly about provisioning for family (and for men then, actually making sure that wives and kids survive) and not really so much about distributing through groups for generalized high status (that helps men in war alliances, to rise in social status, to marry earlier, etc), and even when distributing through groups, not vastly preferentially to males so strongly.<br /> <br /> Although of course you can say then, still, perhaps they give more food to sons than daughters, so there's still that.<br /> <br /> I've probably posted too much on this by this point! This is all a really interesting topic, but is getting away from the purpose of the thread (which is about gender role behaviour in Exalted, which is a good and useful topic in its own right and probably not worth crowding out).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-03T13:58:11-07:00","id":1378341,"title":"","content":"Yes, Saur Ops. Those measurements come with various complications.<br /> But, again, Complications are not Impossibilities. Please do not make an appeal to ignorance. Those variables <i>can</i> be detected and accounted far more than you seem to believe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}